{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>Many days.</b> These are the seven days of his mourning. And one should not wonder that Scripture calls them 'many,' since even three days are called 'many,' as the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Leviticus 15:25): ״ואשה כי יזוב זוב דמה ימים רבים״ — 'days' means two, 'many' means three." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'Many days' — twenty-two years, from when Joseph parted from him until Jacob went down to Egypt. How so? Joseph was seventeen when he parted from his father, and it is written: ״ויוסף בן שלשים שנה בעמדו לפני פרעה״, and seven years of plenty, and it is written: ״כי זה שנתים הרעב״, when Jacob came to Egypt — that makes twenty-two. He suffered over Joseph for twenty-two years, corresponding to the twenty-two years during which Jacob did not fulfill the honor of father and mother: they are twenty years with Laban — fourteen for his two daughters and six years for the flock — and two years on the road: a year and a half in Sukkot and six months in Beit El."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:27.419311886Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}